 12 hours  in time-restricted feeding up to a  24-hour fast  seen in alternate day fasting and  intermittent fasting also includes the  5-2 diets  whereby there's a five-day eating period  with a two-day fast or two days where  there's a very low calorie consumption  and so as you can see in this table here  there are a variety of different  intermittent fasting diets and so in  contrast  periodic fasting refers to a prolonged  and severely calorie  or water-only fasting periods lasting in  most cases between  48 hours in a week but longer time  periods up to 21 days have been  investigated  and so the key difference between  periodic fasting and intermittent  fasting  is that periodic fasting can occur at  specific intervals  or like a kind of as needed basis and is  usually carried out less than once  every two weeks and the two major  methods of periodic fasting  include a water-only periodic fast or a  so-called fasting mimicking diet  that was originally tamed by filter  which is a plant-based caloric  restricted diet containing low proteins  low sugar and high unsaturated fats  that are able to mimic the same effects  as fasting only with water  so what are these effects well the major  source of energy in our bodies is  glucose  and the majority of glucose is stored in  our liver where it is stored as glycogen  when there's increased physical activity  or at the onset  of a fast this liver glycogen gets  broken down into glucose that's so that  it can be used as a fuel of energy  once the liver glycogen is depleted  other sources are used instead  this includes pyruvate and fat derived  glycerol and  amino acids that occur in a process  known as gluconeogenesis  and then as the fasting continues there  is the breakdown of fatty acids for  energy  and this breakdown of fatty acids is  referred to as  ketogenesis and effectively it's a  metabolic pathway that produces  so-called ketone bodies  which is an alternative form of energy  for the body and so during fasting there  effectively is a metabolic switch  from liver-derived glucose to adipose  derived ketone bodies  and so typically the level of ketone  bodies in plasma is around  100 to 250 micromolars however this can  increase up to one millimolar during a  prolonged fast however due to the acidic  nature of the ketone bodies  acetyl acetate and beta hydroxybutyrate  the high levels of ketone bodies around  20 millimolars  can result in keto acidosis which is not  something that you want  so why would there potentially be  benefits from fasting  well this alternative metabolic pathway  is thought to  be focused more on being able to  conserve energy  and to protect the organism while  enduring extended periods of food  deprivation  and this is to optimize survival and  reproduction once food becomes available  again  in terms of the molecular mechanisms a  reduction in the amino acids and glucose  present  reduces the signaling through mtor known  as  the mammalian target of rapamycin that  promotes  growth within a cell moreover a  reduction in glucose reduces signaling  through the rast pathway  that also promotes growth in addition to  amino acids and glucose  insulin levels also reduce during  fasting along with igf-1  which is the insulin like growth factor  moreover  indirect effects of fasting alter the  ratio of different metabolites  including the ratio of coenzyme to  acetyl coa  nadplus to nadh and amp  to atp and these metabolites can affect  directly or indirectly the epigenetic  marks within a cell  for example it can increase search one  activity due to an increase in the  levels of nad plus  and this can instead promote the nacelle  stress resistance pathways  along with an up regulation of  progeostasis and autophagy  and so this all helps enable cells to be  able to survive  when nutrients are scarce and instead  help to recycle  and repair damaged cellular components  and so reasons why this might be  beneficial  comes in line with all of the previous  data i've spoken about in this channel  regarding different potential  supplements  such as rapamycin that inhibits mtor  along with resveratrol that activates  sertoins such as cert-1  and nad-plus precursors that also  help to increase certain one activity  and inhibiting or activating these  different proteins  have been shown in a variety of  different mortal organisms to have  beneficial impacts in terms of both  health span and lifespan  i'd say maybe unsurprisingly then this  metabolic switch that occurs during  fasting  and its alteration in the intracellular  signaling pathways  has been shown in studies to extend  lifespan of bacteria  yeast worms and mice and so this review  article doesn't really touch on these  different supplements  but i will briefly talk about them at  the end and their implications with  fasting  in particular in terms of the re-feeding  process so now let's take a look more  specifically  at the benefits of fasting and in  particular try and try to compare  between  intermittent fasting and periodic  fasting  and interestingly um sachin panda is  in my mind one of the major researchers  in terms of intermittent fasting  whereas volta lungo seems to focus more  on his research on periodic fasting  styles just sound interesting anyway so  i initially  learned about fasting by reading the  circadian code by sachin pandor  and so he talks a lot about  time-restricted feeding which is one of  these forms of intermittent fasting  and so understanding the benefits of  that tries to bring in the  importance of food timing with the  circadian rhythm  and how different processes within our  bodies happen at certain times of the  day  and how eating plays into that and the  study that  always comes to mind when i think about  the benefits of time-restricted feeding  is this cell metabolism paper  time-restricted feeding without reducing  calorie intake prevents metabolic  diseases in mice fed in a high-fat diet  so despite eating the same amount of  calories mice on the time-restricted  feeding diets  whereby they were eating for eight hours  per day were protected against  obesity hyperinsulinema inflammation and  they also had improved motor  coordination  compared to mice that were just eating  adlibitum and they're going to show that  this effect was potentially mediated  by enhanced oscillations of the  circadian clock and the target gene  expression and so given the beneficial  effects seen in this study they then  went and studied  time-restricted feeding in patients with  metabolic syndrome  and they also showed similar cardiac  metabolic benefits  for example the time restricted feeding  prevented excessive body weight gain  improved sleep and attenuated age and  diet induced deterioration in cardiac  performance  and it also improved blood pressure a  different study that looked at the  benefits of the 5-2  intermittent fasting diet showed that  comparing overweight women that either  followed the 5-2 diet or a 25  calorie restricted diet they lost the  same amount of weight  but those in the 5 2 diet had a greater  increase in insulin sensitivity  so there seems to be reported benefits  of intermittent fasting  however there's also studies that do  suggest that the risk of gallstone  disease  nearly doubles between women who fast  for eight hours per day  and those who fast for over 14 hours per  day although this study is quite old and  was quite a small sample size  however it should definitely be further  investigated  moreover another study showed that  skipping breakfast which could be used  as a way of following time-restricted  feeding  is associated with an increased risk of  mortality from cardiovascular and  all-course mortality in the u.s  population  i say more on from that it would be very  interesting to understand  how time-restricted feeding benefits can  vary depending on whether the feeding  occurred  during the morning or evening so whether  or not breakfast time  or 