he mice was  that although they were calorie  restricted during the diet  they compensated for the restriction by  overeating  in the ad libertarian period and falter  goes on to say in this review article  about the importance of the potential  importance of this refeeding process  as it seems the cycles of fasting and  then re-feeding  are powerful promoters of stem cell cell  renewal mechanisms  and activators of tissue regeneration in  part through inhibition  and reactivation of igf-1 and  mtor pathways so the idea that you first  inhibit these pathways  to promote stress resistance and then  when food  returns those pathways get suppressed  and growth pathways instead get  signaling  that allows the regeneration of new  tissue  and the evidence for this that was shown  in this 2015 paper by photo is nicely  summarized in this review figure where  by the fmd diet affected tissue  regeneration  in immune cells bone cells brain cells  muscle cells pancreatic cells as well as  in the intestine  and it's thought that this regeneration  in part helps to extend the health span  of these mice  because it protects the mice against  age-related decline in these different  tissues  but the important question or at least  well for me the thing that i find  interesting is how this is happening  is the changes in the metabolic  signaling having epigenetic changes  or is it changing the stem cell niche  which regulates the activity of the stem  cells  and so as folta says the role of  regeneration during the refeeding period  is poorly understood and should be  investigated further in multiple tissues  and the other reason why i think that  this is quite interesting in addition to  just  understanding the importance of fast  mimicking diets  is due to the fact that these different  supplements that have been shown to  extend  lifespan in different muscle organisms  such as rapamycin  and resveratrol these are acting to  inhibit the signaling through these  growth pathways  and so what if it's the case that the  best thing to do is to have  periods of inhibition of grave signaling  followed by activation so actually  trying to activate mtall signaling  although i guess the best way to do that  really is just by food but yeah  interesting questions um we shall have  to await answers  and i didn't say but there have been  human studies that have looked into the  impact of fmd  for example in one study there was a  randomized clinical trial with a hundred  relatively healthy volunteers that used  fmd cycles lasting five days  carried out once per month for three  months and it was shown to reduce  multiple risk factors for age-related  diseases  including diabetes cancer and  cardiovascular disease  however i'm going to emphasize what  falter says in this review that although  fmds appear to be safe  their use should be limited to three  times per year in healthy people with  normal levels of disease risk factors  until additional and long-term clinical  studies demonstrating the safety of more  frequent use are carried out  and so this comes back to another  question of which fasting mechanism  would potentially be more effective  periodic fasting such as fmd or  intermittent fasting such as time  restricted feeding  and the thing is we don't really know  enough about irithm or have enough  studies to be able to directly compare  them  and maybe i've potentially been swayed  by falters  biased opinions on fmd diets it does  seem very interesting this refeeding  process  and so maybe there is some truth and the  fact that  these more severe fasting refeeding  do have a better kind of stop-start  action  on the regenerative processes as opposed  to chronic  time-restricted feeding but again we  just don't have the answers yet  and the other area where researchers  really required is also looking at  the age dependent differences in these  different fasting approaches  in a different to different sex  dependent differences in particular for 